\section{Experiments}\label{sec:experiments}

\subsection{Experimental Setup}\label{sec:setup}

\paragraph{Datasets.}
We evaluate on four English NER benchmarks spanning a difficulty gradient.
\textbf{SciERC} \cite{luan2018scierc} (551 test instances, 6 entity types) is the primary medium-difficulty test bed (${\approx}$65\% F1).
\textbf{Few-NERD} \cite{ding2021fewnerd} (INTRA coarse; 37{,}648 instances, 8 entity types, ${\approx}$75\% F1) provides a low-degeneracy contrast where LP selection succeeds (\S\ref{sec:selection_gap}).
\textbf{CoNLL-2003} \cite{tjongkimsang2003conll} (3{,}453 test sentences, 4 entity types, ${\approx}$91\% F1) serves as a ceiling-effect control.
\textbf{WNUT-17} \cite{derczynski2017wnut} (1{,}287 social media instances, 51\% zero F1) probes the signal-collapse boundary.

\paragraph{Models and sampling.}
\textbf{Qwen3-8B} and \textbf{LLaMA-3.1-8B-Instruct} are LoRA fine-tuned using LLaMA Factory \cite{zheng2024llamafactory} (Appendix~\ref{app:training}; Few-NERD uses 3 epochs vs.\ 5 for others; Appendix~\ref{app:epoch_confound}).
$N{=}8$ samples at $T{=}1.0$ (primary) with vLLM constrained decoding \cite{willard2023guided}; $N{=}16$ for scaling (Appendix~\ref{app:n_scaling}).

%% ============================================================
\subsection{Quality Estimation Results}\label{sec:qe_results}

\begin{table*}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{NER quality estimation ($\rho$ and AUROC; $N{=}8$, primary seed${=}42$; 3-seed $\sigma{\leq}0.026$ in Table~\ref{tab:multiseed}). AUROC binarizes at per-dataset median F1. Best in \textbf{bold}. RE in Appendix~\ref{app:re_extension}; $N{=}16$ in Table~\ref{tab:robustness}.}
\label{tab:quality_estimation}
\begin{tabular}{ll cc cc cc}
\toprule
& & \multicolumn{2}{c}{\textbf{SciERC}} & \multicolumn{2}{c}{\textbf{CoNLL (Qwen)}} & \multicolumn{2}{c}{\textbf{CoNLL (LLaMA)}} \\
\cmidrule(lr){3-4} \cmidrule(lr){5-6} \cmidrule(lr){7-8}
Signal & Family & $\rho$ & AUC & $\rho$ & AUC & $\rho$ & AUC \\
\midrule
VC & Structural & \textbf{.379} & .684 & .424 & .789 & .426 & .709 \\
SJ  & Structural & .360 & .692 & .436 & .801 & \textbf{.431} & .704 \\
FK & Surface & .267 & .629 & .400 & .774 & .428 & .703 \\
EM  & Surface    & .295 & \textbf{.749} & \textbf{.451} & \textbf{.816} & \textbf{.431} & .705 \\
\midrule
LP     & Model      & .205 & .578 & .316 & .753 & .307 & \textbf{.761} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table*}

Table~\ref{tab:quality_estimation} presents $\rho$ and AUROC for five signals across tasks and models.

\paragraph{Signal rankings are difficulty-dependent.}
On SciERC, structural signals lead (VC $\rho{=}0.379$, SJ $0.360$) over LP ($0.205$); bootstrap CIs confirm the gap ($\Delta\rho{=}{+}0.155$; Appendix~\ref{app:bootstrap_signal}).
On CoNLL (F1${\approx}$0.91), EM leads both $\rho$ and AUROC ($0.816$).
At the signal-collapse boundary (WNUT-17, F1${\approx}$42\%), full-set correlations are dominated by the zero/non-zero boundary (VC $\rho{=}0.724$ at $N{=}16$, but conditional $\rho{=}0.038$; Table~\ref{tab:wnut}); LP shows the reverse (conditional $\rho{=}0.350$; Figure~\ref{fig:signal_heatmap}).

%% ============================================================
\subsection{Cross-Dataset Contrast: Few-NERD}\label{sec:fewnerd}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{LP selection across datasets (Qwen3-8B, $N{=}8$, $T{=}1.0$, gold-filtered). Few-NERD/CoNLL: 3-seed means; SciERC: single-seed. $p$: paired bootstrap.}
\label{tab:fewnerd}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{@{}lrccccr@{}}
\toprule
Dataset & $n$ & Greedy & Degen.\% & LP $\rho$ & LP $\Delta$ & $p$ \\
\midrule
WNUT-17 & 1{,}287 & .452 & 67.9\% & $-$.446 & $-$0.45\,pp & .72 \\
CoNLL & 2{,}756 & .908 & 61.7\% & .302 & $-$0.85\,pp & 1.0 \\
SciERC & 529 & .644 & 17.8\% & .205 & $+$0.61\,pp & .193 \\
Few-NERD & 32{,}565 & .748 & 13.0\% & .220 & $\mathbf{+1.39}$\,pp & ${<}.001$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\end{table}

Few-NERD (Table~\ref{tab:fewnerd}) confirms LP selection at low degeneracy ($+1.39$~pp, $p{<}0.001$; structural signals degrade: VC $-1.79$, SJ $-2.14$~pp), with LP 50$\times$ more seed-stable ($\sigma{=}0.04$ vs.\ SJ $2.02$~pp), generalizing to LLaMA (Table~\ref{tab:cross_selection}). Training convergence modulates degeneracy (5-epoch: $31$\%, halving LP gains; Appendix~\ref{app:epoch_confound}).

\paragraph{Degeneracy alone does not predict LP direction.}
Across 23 configurations, degeneracy does not predict LP gain ($\rho{=}0.052$, $p{=}0.81$; Figure~\ref{fig:degeneracy_scatter}), though it causally modulates LP within fixed settings ($\rho{=}{-}0.881$; Appendix~\ref{app:per_type_lp}). Cross-dataset prediction requires LP signal quality (Appendix~\ref{app:conditional_decomp}).

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_degeneracy_lp_scatter.pdf}
\caption{Instance degeneracy vs.\ LP selection gain across 23 configurations ($N{=}8$; $\rho{=}0.052$, $p{=}0.81$). Within Few-NERD per-entity-type ($\rho{=}{-}0.881$; Appendix~\ref{app:per_type_lp}), degeneracy predicts LP gain when alignment is held constant.}
\label{fig:degeneracy_scatter}
\end{figure}

Structural signals remain negative regardless of sample budget; $N$-scaling analyses are in Appendix~\ref{app:n_scaling}.

%% ============================================================
\subsection{Robustness}\label{sec:robustness_main}

Consistency signals are robust across $T{\in}\{0.7, 1.0\}$ with sublinear $N$-scaling gains (Table~\ref{tab:robustness}); signal rankings are stable across seeds ($W{=}1.0$; Table~\ref{tab:ranking-stability}). NER calibration is reasonable (ECE 0.069--0.096; Appendix~\ref{app:calibration}).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Scale ablation: 8B vs.\ 4B QE $\rho$ ($N{=}8$, $T{=}1.0$, seed 42). RE in Appendix~\ref{app:re_extension}.}
\label{tab:scale_ablation}
\begin{tabular}{@{}ll cc r@{}}
\toprule
& & \textbf{8B} & \textbf{4B} & $\Delta$ \\
\midrule
CoNLL & SJ & .436 & .422 & $-$.014 \\
      & FK & .400 & .418 & $+$.018 \\
SciERC & SJ & .360 & .422 & $+$.062 \\
       & FK & .267 & .326 & $+$.059 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Scale robustness.}
With Qwen3-4B (Table~\ref{tab:scale_ablation}), QE is comparable (mean $|\Delta\rho|{=}0.042$), yet selection remains below greedy despite 13.0~pp headroom. Task difficulty is a stronger predictor than model scale within the 3B--8B range (Appendix~\ref{app:llama3b}).
Degeneracy scales with capability: 8B shows 17.8\% vs.\ 4B's 10.4\% on SciERC (Table~\ref{tab:degeneracy}).

\paragraph{Controlled ablations.}
LoRA rank${=}8$ (vs.\ 32) lowers degeneracy to 7.2\% but LP remains below greedy ($\rho{\approx}0.24$), confirming low degeneracy and adequate alignment are independently necessary ($T{=}1.2$; \S\ref{sec:discussion}).
Supervised classifiers, a DeBERTa verifier, and hidden state probes all fail to close the gap (Appendix~\ref{app:supervised_combination}, \ref{app:hidden_probe}).

%% ============================================================
\subsection{Prescriptive Results}\label{sec:prescriptive}

Since instance-level selection fails, we present approaches that extract value from multi-sample generation without sample ranking.

\paragraph{Entity-level construction.}
We aggregate entities across samples, retaining those exceeding $\theta{=}2/N$, with LP-weighted or uniform scoring ($\theta$ stable in $[0.15, 0.35]$; Appendix~\ref{app:theta_sweep}). In zero-shot, LP-weighting is necessary (SciERC $+1.5$\,pp; \S\ref{sec:scaling}).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Entity construction vs.\ greedy ($\Delta$ macro F1; $N{=}8$, $T{=}1.0$, Qwen3-8B, multi-seed mean${\pm}\sigma$). $\theta{=}2/N$. $p$: paired bootstrap vs.\ greedy. $^\dagger$Survives Holm--Bonferroni ($k{=}57$). $^\ddagger$LP-weighted not computed.}
\label{tab:entity_construction}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{@{}llcccc@{}}
\toprule
Dataset & Wt. & Greedy & Constr. & $\Delta$ & $p$ \\
\midrule
SciERC & LP & $.646{\pm}.002$ & $\mathbf{.656{\pm}.003}$ & $+1.02{\pm}0.49$\,pp & .004 \\
       & Unif. & $.646{\pm}.002$ & $.653{\pm}.006$ & $+0.74{\pm}0.71$\,pp & .023 \\
\addlinespace
Few-NERD & LP & $.748$ & $\mathbf{.762{\pm}.005}$ & $+1.40{\pm}0.51$\,pp & ${<}.001^\dagger$ \\
         & Unif. & $.748$ & $.756{\pm}.006$ & $+0.74{\pm}0.58$\,pp & .003 \\
\addlinespace
CoNLL$^\ddagger$ & Unif. & .908 & .904 & $-0.39$\,pp & n.s. \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\end{table}

LP-weighted construction improves on Few-NERD ($+1.40{\pm}0.51$\,pp, $p{<}0.001$; survives Holm--Bonferroni at $k{=}57$) and SciERC ($+1.02{\pm}0.49$\,pp, $p{=}0.004$, $p_{\text{adj}}{=}0.20$; Table~\ref{tab:entity_construction}), recovering 8--11\% of oracle headroom. CoNLL shows no gain ($-0.39$\,pp), consistent with high degeneracy ($N$-scaling in Appendix~\ref{app:n_scaling}).

\paragraph{LP-alignment gating.}
Construction and LP selection are complementary, governed by $\rho_{\text{adj}}$: Few-NERD ($\rho_{\text{adj}}{=}0.516$) favors both, while SciERC ($\rho_{\text{adj}}{=}0.167$) favors construction only. A three-way routing protocol ($\rho_{\text{adj}} \geq 0.3$: LP; $<0.3$ with positive construction: construction; else: greedy) is stable across $\tau{\in}[0.2, 0.4]$.

\paragraph{Selective prediction.}
QE signals support selective prediction: at 80\% coverage, filtering low-confidence instances yields $+5$--$8$\,pp F1 (AUPRC 0.70--0.92; Table~\ref{tab:cross_selection}).

\phantomsection\label{sec:selection_results}

\section{Introduction}\label{sec:introduction}

Test-time compute scaling has emerged as a complement to model scaling \cite{snell2025scaling}: repeated sampling with majority voting \citep{wang2023selfconsistency}, best-of-$N$ with learned verifiers \cite{cobbe2021verifiers, brown2024monkeys}, and extended chain-of-thought \cite{muennighoff2025s1} yield large gains on reasoning and code generation, but the evidence concentrates on tasks with discrete answers or execution-based verification.
Named entity recognition (NER) poses a structurally different challenge: the output is a \textit{set} of entities, two samples may extract overlapping but non-identical sets, and there is no obvious majority to vote on.
This challenge generalizes to any \textit{set-valued} structured prediction task where outputs admit partial but not exact overlap.

Prior work on confidence estimation for LLM-based IE has conflated \textit{quality estimation} (which inputs does the model handle reliably?) with \textit{sample selection} (which of $N$ outputs is best?).
Existing self-consistency methods \cite{wang2023selfconsistency, chen2024usc, taubenfeld2025cisc, kang2025selfcertainty} assume outputs can be grouped by equivalence, an assumption that breaks for entity sets with partial overlap.
Unlike \citet{yao2026consistency}, who demonstrate that self-consistency correlates with task accuracy, we provide the first systematic empirical characterization of \textit{when and why} test-time scaling via LP-based selection succeeds or fails for structured prediction, diagnosing degeneracy and compression as the two blocking mechanisms.

A key distinction structures our analysis: four of five signals (Soft Jaccard, voting confidence, Fleiss' $\kappa$, exact match) are instance-level aggregates that assign identical scores to all $N$ candidates for a given input; only log-probability (LP) is natively per-sample.
All signals achieve reliable quality estimation ($\rho$ up to 0.45, AUROC up to 0.82; Figure~\ref{fig:overview}), supporting selective prediction.
For selection, LP achieves $+1.39$~pp on Few-NERD ($p{<}0.001$) in settings where both low instance degeneracy and adequate alignment are present, with gains scaling with $N$.
On SciERC, the observed LP gain ($+0.2$~pp, $p{=}0.29$) falls below the minimum detectable effect (${\approx}$1.5~pp at 80\% power, $n{=}529$) despite 13~pp oracle headroom. We term this disparity between quality estimation strength and selection effectiveness the \textbf{correlation-selection gap}.
Three factors shape this gap: instance degeneracy (13--68\%), oracle headroom, and LP-quality alignment (\S\ref{sec:selection_gap}).
Oracle F1 scales comparably to reasoning ($c{\approx}0.5$; \citealp{brown2024monkeys}), yet aggregation recovers ${<}1$\,pp---a persistent \textit{aggregation gap} (\S\ref{sec:scaling}) that our diagnosis traces to degeneracy eliminating candidate diversity before utility alignment matters \cite{eikema2025structured}.

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{fig_overview}
\caption{Overview of the correlation-selection gap. \textbf{Left}: consistency signals estimate instance quality ($\rho$ up to 0.45; Table~\ref{tab:quality_estimation}). \textbf{Center}: instance-level signals assign identical scores to all $N$ candidates, so selection cannot outperform greedy. \textbf{Right}: entity-level construction via majority vote ($\theta{=}2/N$) bypasses the bottleneck ($+1.40$\,pp on Few-NERD; \S\ref{sec:prescriptive}).}
\label{fig:overview}
\end{figure*}

We evaluate five signals on four NER benchmarks (SciERC \cite{luan2018scierc}, Few-NERD \cite{ding2021fewnerd}, CoNLL-2003 \cite{tjongkimsang2003conll}, WNUT-17 \cite{derczynski2017wnut}) using two 8B models with a 4B ablation, varying $N{\in}\{2,\ldots,32\}$ across 4B--8B fine-tuned and 72B zero-shot regimes.
Our contributions:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Quality estimation as a test-time scaling diagnostic.}
    Five signals from three families evaluated on four NER benchmarks, two model families, and a 4B ablation, achieving reliable quality estimation ($\rho$ up to 0.45, AUROC up to 0.82).
    QE enables selective prediction: filtering low-confidence instances at 80\% coverage yields $+5$--$8$\,pp F1 (AUPRC 0.70--0.92, VC strongest; \S\ref{sec:prescriptive}).

    \item \textbf{Failure modes of test-time scaling for structured prediction.}
    LP-quality alignment is the primary predictor of selection success ($+1.39$~pp on Few-NERD, $p{<}0.001$); instance degeneracy causally modulates LP within fixed settings but does not predict cross-dataset direction ($\rho{=}0.052$, 23 configs).
    The resulting three-step diagnostic provides a reusable protocol for structured test-time scaling (\S\ref{sec:discussion}).

    \item \textbf{Entity-level construction as a partial mitigation.}
    Adapting entity voting from ensemble NER \cite{florian2003named} to stochastic sampling, LP-weighted construction yields $+1.40$\,pp on Few-NERD ($p{<}0.001$; survives Holm--Bonferroni) and $+1.02$\,pp on SciERC ($p{=}0.004$; \S\ref{sec:prescriptive}).

    \item \textbf{A degeneracy decomposition of SC gain.}
    We decompose $\mathbb{E}[\Delta F_1] = (1{-}D) \cdot G_{\text{nd}} \cdot \alpha$, yielding a critical threshold $\theta_{\text{crit}}{=}0.70$ above which SC cannot yield significant improvement (\S\ref{sec:theory}).
\end{enumerate}

\section{Discussion}\label{sec:discussion}

Structured IE exhibits oracle scaling comparable to reasoning ($c{\approx}0.5$; \S\ref{sec:scaling}), but aggregation cannot close the gap: degeneracy (17.8--67.9\%), limited headroom, and LP-quality alignment ($\rho$ 0.043--0.667) constrain it.
Temperature disentangles these at $T{=}1.2$ (degeneracy 14.9\%, LP $\rho{=}0.225$; Table~\ref{tab:t_degeneracy}).

\paragraph{From diagnosis to practice.}\label{sec:selection_method}
Entity-level construction bypasses both failure mechanisms, with gains positive when degeneracy ${<}22$\% and absent when ${>}31$\% (all $p{<}0.01$; Appendix~\ref{app:epoch_confound}). Under 72B few-shot, construction fails yet QE remains effective (SJ $\rho$ 0.29--0.44; Appendix~\ref{app:fs_error}).
Alternative strategies (DALS, MBR, DGS) and supervised mixtures fail to beat greedy (Appendix~\ref{app:dals}, \ref{app:supervised_combination}, \ref{app:when_selection_works}).

\section{Theory \& Scaling Law}\label{sec:theory_scaling}

\subsection{Entity Construction as MBR Decoding}\label{sec:mbr_theory}

Entity-level construction (\S\ref{sec:prescriptive}) is a special case of minimum Bayes risk (MBR) decoding \cite{kumar2004minimum, eikema2020map}, extended from candidate selection ($\arg\max_{y \in \{y_1,\ldots,y_N\}}$) to construction over all subsets of $E_\cup{=}\bigcup_i y_i$.

\begin{proposition}[Entity construction as MBR for balanced set-overlap]\label{prop:construction_mbr}
Under entity-wise independence with utility $u(\hat{y}, y') = |\hat{y} \cap y'| - \alpha|\hat{y} \setminus y'|$ ($\alpha \geq 1$), the MBR-optimal constructed set is
\begin{equation}\label{eq:mbr_construction}
\hat{y}_{\textup{MBR}} \!=\! \bigl\{e \!{\in}\! E_\cup \!:\! \hat{p}(e) \!>\! \tfrac{\alpha}{\alpha{+}1}\bigr\},\; \hat{p}(e) \!=\! \tfrac{1}{N} \textstyle\sum\nolimits_{i} \mathbf{1}[e \!{\in}\! y_i]
\end{equation}
The special case $\alpha{=}1$ yields $\hat{p}(e) > \tfrac{1}{2}$, the majority-vote threshold used throughout (proof in Appendix~\ref{app:mbr_proof}).
\end{proposition}

\noindent\textbf{Remark.}
Set-level F1 is non-linear and admits no closed-form threshold rule \cite{dembczynski2011exact}. We use balanced linear utility ($\alpha{=}1$ throughout); balanced set-overlap and F1 induce similar optimal thresholds for typical NER output sizes ($|E_\cup| \leq 30$).

Signal-derived weights $w_i$ yield quality-weighted MBR: $\hat{p}_w(e) = \sum_{i} w_i \, \mathbf{1}[e {\in} y_i] / \sum_{i} w_i$.
Weighted MBR improves when $w_i$ correlates with $u(y_i, y^*)$ within instances; when this correlation is weak (LP on SciERC: $\rho{\approx}0.2$; few-shot: \S\ref{sec:scaling}), weighting degrades (\S\ref{sec:prescriptive}).

\subsection{Scaling Behavior}\label{sec:scaling}

We test whether the gap persists across scales and model families using Qwen2.5 (7B fine-tuned, 72B zero-shot and few-shot) alongside the primary Qwen3-8B.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Cross-scale and cross-family comparison ($N{=}8$, $T{=}1.0$). Hdr.\ = oracle $-$ greedy (pp). Degen.: instance degeneracy (\%). $^\star$Qwen2.5; unmarked: Qwen3. $^\dagger$Not computed.}
\label{tab:scale_comparison}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{@{}llccccccc@{}}
\toprule
Dataset & Regime & Greedy & Hdr. & Degen. & VC & SJ & FK & LP \\
\midrule
SciERC & 8B FT  & .644 & 13.0 & 17.8 & .379 & .360 & .267 & .205 \\
       & 72B ZS$^\star$ & .399 & 15.0 & 21.0 & .277 & .217 & .227 & .081 \\
\midrule
Few-NERD & 7B FT$^\star$ & .784 & 9.2 & 31.7 & .404 & .388 & .315 & .346 \\
         & 8B FT & .748 & 12.4 & 13.0 & .386 & .300 & .313 & .220 \\
         & 72B ZS$^\star$ & .628 & ---$^\dagger$ & 46.2 & .416 & .345 & ---$^\dagger$ & .119 \\
\midrule
CoNLL  & 8B FT  & .908 & ${\approx}$4 & 61.7 & .424 & .436 & .400 & .302 \\
       & 72B ZS$^\star$ & .712 & 5.3 & 67.7 & .297 & .304 & .302 & $-$.002 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\end{table}

LP $\rho$ drops without fine-tuning (Table~\ref{tab:scale_comparison}) and LP selection fails on all datasets. Structural signals generalize (Few-NERD VC $\rho{=}0.416$). Degeneracy increases (Few-NERD: $46.2$\% vs.\ $13.0$\%; CoNLL: $67.7$\% vs.\ $61.7$\%); this comparison simultaneously varies model family, regime, and quantization, so individual effects cannot be disentangled.
LP-weighted construction yields $+1.53$\,pp on SciERC 72B ZS ($p{=}0.008$, CI [$+0.25$, $+2.82$]), recovering $10.2$\% of headroom. Construction fails on Few-NERD ($-0.20$\,pp) and CoNLL ($-0.37$\,pp) where degeneracy exceeds $46$\%---consistent with FT-regime degeneracy gating.

\paragraph{Few-shot demonstrations improve QE but not construction.}
Adding 3-shot demonstrations to 72B improves greedy on SciERC and CoNLL ($+3.3$--$6.2$\,pp) but not Few-NERD ($-0.9$\,pp), and strengthens structural QE (CoNLL SJ $\rho$ $.304{\to}.442$, LP $-.002{\to}.106$). Yet construction is ineffective across all three datasets (SciERC $-0.08$\,pp, CoNLL $+0.03$\,pp, Few-NERD $+0.02$\,pp; all n.s.). On SciERC, this failure despite low degeneracy shows the bottleneck is regime-dependent (Appendix~\ref{app:fs_error}); on Few-NERD, high FS degeneracy ($45.2$\%) places it in the expected failure regime.

\paragraph{Degeneracy is model-family-dependent, not scale-dependent.}
Qwen2.5 exhibits systematically higher degeneracy than Qwen3 on Few-NERD (7B--72B: $31.7$--$46.2$\% vs.\ Qwen3-8B: $13.0$\%; Table~\ref{tab:scale_comparison}), confirming degeneracy is shaped by architecture rather than parameter count. Construction fails for all Qwen2.5 configurations on Few-NERD (both n.s.); 3$\to$5 epochs triples degeneracy within the same model (\S\ref{sec:fewnerd}).

Oracle F1 follows $F_1(N) = a - b \cdot N^{-c}$ ($R^2 > 0.999$; Figure~\ref{fig:oracle_scaling}): FewNERD ($c{\approx}0.61$) scales comparably to reasoning tasks \cite{brown2024monkeys}, while SciERC converges more slowly ($c{\approx}0.35$); a single stochastic sample underperforms greedy ($N{\geq}4$ needed to match).
The ceiling grows---but confidence-based aggregation recovers ${<}1$\,pp, establishing a persistent aggregation gap.

\subsection{Degeneracy Decomposition}\label{sec:theory}

\noindent\textbf{Degeneracy Decomposition.}\label{thm:degeneracy_bound}
For any aggregation method $A$: $\mathbb{E}[\Delta F_1(A)] \leq (1 - D) \cdot G_{\textup{nd}}$,
where $D$ is the degeneracy rate and $G_{\textup{nd}}$ the oracle gap on non-degenerate instances.
For signal-based methods with alignment $\alpha \in [0,1]$, this tightens to $(1{-}D) \cdot G_{\textup{nd}} \cdot \alpha$ (proof in Appendix~\ref{app:bound_proof}).

\noindent With $G_{\textup{nd}}{\approx}14.8$\,pp and $\alpha{\approx}0.068$, the critical threshold is $\theta_{\textup{crit}}{=}0.70$: when $D{>}70$\%, SC gain falls below $0.3$\,pp.
Across 16 configurations, all gains respect the bound (ratio 3--12\%; Figure~\ref{fig:bound}); the bound is loose because $\alpha$---not $D$---is the bottleneck.

\section{Discussion}\label{sec:discussion}

Structured IE exhibits oracle scaling comparable to reasoning ($c{\approx}0.5$; \S\ref{sec:scaling}), but aggregation cannot close the gap: degeneracy (17.8--67.9\%), limited headroom, and LP-quality alignment ($\rho$ 0.043--0.667) constrain it.
Temperature disentangles these at $T{=}1.2$ (degeneracy 14.9\%, LP $\rho{=}0.225$; Table~\ref{tab:t_degeneracy}).

\paragraph{From diagnosis to practice.}\label{sec:selection_method}
Entity-level construction bypasses both failure mechanisms, with gains positive when degeneracy ${<}22$\% and absent when ${>}31$\% (all $p{<}0.01$; Appendix~\ref{app:epoch_confound}). Under 72B few-shot, construction fails yet QE remains effective (SJ $\rho$ 0.29--0.44; Appendix~\ref{app:fs_error}).
Alternative strategies (DALS, MBR, DGS) and supervised mixtures fail to beat greedy (Appendix~\ref{app:dals}, \ref{app:supervised_combination}, \ref{app:when_selection_works}).

