\section{Why Single-Signal Selection Fails}\label{sec:selection_gap}

\paragraph{Definitional vs.\ empirical.}
That four of five signals assign identical scores to all candidates from the same input is definitional (\S\ref{sec:formulation}): instance-level aggregates estimate quality across inputs but cannot rank candidates within an instance.
The empirical findings are twofold: (a)~LP---the only per-sample signal---suffers compression under constrained decoding (median within-instance range 0.041 nats; 60\% of SciERC instances below this threshold; Appendix~\ref{app:logprob_ties}), and (b)~even where LP varies, selection gains depend on LP-quality alignment rather than degeneracy alone.
Cross-instance $\rho$ (Table~\ref{tab:quality_estimation}) is attenuated by varying instance difficulty; within-instance $\rho$ directly measures whether LP discriminates candidates for a fixed input, making it the relevant predictor of selection success.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Best-of-$N$ selection (macro F1) on SciERC ($N{=}8$, $T{=}1.0$, 2-seed mean${\pm}\sigma$). LP is per-sample; others use consensus-proximity. $^\dagger$One score per input.}
\label{tab:selection}
\begin{tabular}{lcc}
\toprule
Method & NER F1 & $p$ \\
\midrule
Oracle & $0.775 \pm 0.001$ & --- \\
Greedy ($T{=}0$) & $0.645 \pm 0.001$ & --- \\
\midrule
LP           & $\mathbf{0.647} \pm 0.005$ & .29 \\
SJ           & $0.646 \pm 0.002$ & .67 \\
FK           & $0.646 \pm 0.001$ & .79 \\
EM           & $0.637 \pm 0.001$ & n/a$^\dagger$ \\
VC           & $0.623 \pm 0.004$ & n/a$^\dagger$ \\
\midrule
Random       & $0.610 \pm 0.003$ & --- \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig_selection_gap}
\caption{Correlation-selection gap on SciERC ($N{=}8$, $T{=}1.0$). Bars: QE $\rho$; line: $\Delta$F1 vs.\ greedy (3-seed mean). Despite $\rho$ up to 0.38, no signal outperforms greedy ($p{>}0.05$). Dashed green: oracle ($+13.0$\,pp); dotted gray: random.}
\label{fig:selection_gap}
\end{figure}

Despite $\rho$ up to 0.38, no signal outperforms greedy on SciERC (Table~\ref{tab:selection}; Figure~\ref{fig:selection_gap}); the oracle reveals 13.0\,pp headroom, but LP achieves $+0.2$\,pp ($p{=}0.29$), below MDE (${\approx}$1.5\,pp; $n{=}529$).
The pattern holds across CoNLL and WNUT-17 (Table~\ref{tab:cross_selection}).
Few-NERD provides the definitive contrast: LP achieves $+1.39$\,pp ($p{<}0.001$) at low degeneracy (13.0\% vs.\ 17.8--67.9\%), while structural signals still degrade (VC $-1.79$, SJ $-2.14$\,pp; \S\ref{sec:fewnerd}).

\paragraph{Three governing factors.}
(1)~Instance degeneracy (SciERC 17.8\%, CoNLL 61.7\%); (2)~oracle headroom (CoNLL ${\approx}4$\,pp vs.\ SciERC 13.0\,pp); (3)~LP-quality alignment (Few-NERD within-instance $\rho{\approx}0.5$--$0.7$ vs.\ SciERC $\rho{\approx}0.26$).
Across 23 configurations, degeneracy does not predict LP gain ($\rho{=}0.052$, $p{=}0.81$; Figure~\ref{fig:degeneracy_scatter}), though it causally modulates LP within fixed settings ($\rho{=}{-}0.881$; Appendix~\ref{app:per_type_lp}).
The factors do not combine into a predictive model ($R^2{<}0$) but each is supported by controlled ablations (\S\ref{sec:fewnerd}, \S\ref{sec:discussion}).

\section{Experiments}\label{sec:experiments}

\subsection{Experimental Setup}\label{sec:setup}

\paragraph{Datasets.}
We evaluate on four English NER benchmarks spanning a difficulty gradient.
\textbf{SciERC} \cite{luan2018scierc} (551 test instances, 6 entity types) is the primary medium-difficulty test bed (${\approx}$65\% F1).
\textbf{Few-NERD} \cite{ding2021fewnerd} (INTRA coarse; 37{,}648 instances, 8 entity types, ${\approx}$75\% F1) provides a low-degeneracy contrast where LP selection succeeds (\S\ref{sec:selection_gap}).
\textbf{CoNLL-2003} \cite{tjongkimsang2003conll} (3{,}453 test sentences, 4 entity types, ${\approx}$91\% F1) serves as a ceiling-effect control.
\textbf{WNUT-17} \cite{derczynski2017wnut} (1{,}287 social media instances, 51\% zero F1) probes the signal-collapse boundary.

\paragraph{Models and sampling.}
\textbf{Qwen3-8B} and \textbf{LLaMA-3.1-8B-Instruct} are LoRA fine-tuned using LLaMA Factory \cite{zheng2024llamafactory} (Appendix~\ref{app:training}; Few-NERD uses 3 epochs vs.\ 5 for others; Appendix~\ref{app:epoch_confound}).
$N{=}8$ samples at $T{=}1.0$ (primary) with vLLM constrained decoding \cite{willard2023guided}; $N{=}16$ for scaling (Appendix~\ref{app:n_scaling}).

%% ============================================================
\subsection{Quality Estimation Results}\label{sec:qe_results}

\begin{table*}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{NER quality estimation ($\rho$ and AUROC; $N{=}8$, primary seed${=}42$; 3-seed $\sigma{\leq}0.026$ in Table~\ref{tab:multiseed}). AUROC binarizes at per-dataset median F1. Best in \textbf{bold}. RE in Appendix~\ref{app:re_extension}; $N{=}16$ in Table~\ref{tab:robustness}.}
\label{tab:quality_estimation}
